1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,587
At a rally in Montana this October, President Trump
told the crowd that "tariffs don't cause inflation".

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:14,038
If he is right, a tariff costs shoppers nothing
extra. If he is wrong, somebody here is paying it.

3
00:00:14,867 --> 00:00:23,679
In his State of the Union, Trump said who he thinks pays: countries that
were ripping us off "are now paying us hundreds of billions of dollars".

4
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:31,700
On his channel, Patrick Boyle puts it the other way round: "A
tariff is just a tax paid by Americans, not by Canadians."

5
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:36,288
They cannot both be right. So follow
the cost, starting at the border.

6
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:45,875
If foreign sellers were paying, they would have cut their prices to make room for the
tariff, so economists at the New York Fed checked their prices with the tariff taken out.

7
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:58,138
In the first eight months of 2025, a 10 percent tariff cut those prices by only 0.6 percentage
points, which by the Fed's estimate left 94 percent of the cost with American importers.

8
00:00:58,167 --> 00:01:05,842
Foreign sellers did start giving more ground late in the
year, yet in November importers still carried 86 percent.

9
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:13,317
But an importer paying at the border is not yet a shopper
paying at the till. How much of it reaches the shelf?

10
00:01:14,133 --> 00:01:28,271
A New York Fed staff report on the 2025 tariffs estimates that consumer prices rise by about 26 percent of the
tariff increase, roughly a quarter of the tariff rate, measured against goods less exposed to the tariffs.

11
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:40,100
And not all of that comes from imports: 36 percent of it comes from goods made at home, because American
producers pay more for imported parts, and with foreign rivals pricier, they raise their markups.

12
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:42,875
Nor does it arrive the day
a tariff is announced.

13
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:55,688
In the New York Fed's estimates, about half of the direct effect on imported goods is in shop prices
after three months, and all of it after six, as retailers take a few months to pass on higher costs.

14
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,413
The part that runs through American-made goods is slower still,
taking nine to twelve months to work through supply chains.

15
00:02:03,433 --> 00:02:17,571
Firms in the New York Fed's business surveys gave their reasons for spreading increases out: some sell under fixed-price
contracts they cannot change until they expire, and some raise prices a little at a time to avoid shocking customers.

16
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:22,913
So is a tariff a one-time bump
in prices, or lasting inflation?

17
00:02:23,933 --> 00:02:41,546
The New York Fed estimates the tariffs' effect on the level of consumer goods prices peaked near 3 percent in February 2026, and forecasts it
to ease to about 2 percent by August after a Supreme Court ruling cut some tariffs, then edge up again with new tariffs on Canadian goods.

18
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:52,179
Its contribution to the yearly rise in consumer goods prices also peaked that
February, at 2.9 percentage points, and is forecast to fall to around zero by August.

19
00:02:52,200 --> 00:03:04,225
But the Fed's survey researchers warn that "one-time" can be "a drawn-out affair, especially when
the tariffs change frequently", with many firms spreading price increases across extended periods.

20
00:03:05,233 --> 00:03:18,358
So who pays? By the Fed's estimates, mostly not foreign sellers: American importers carried the bulk
of the 2025 tariffs, and part of that cost reached consumer prices, including for goods made at home.

21
00:03:18,367 --> 00:03:30,742
What these estimates cannot give you is the economy-wide inflation rate: they compare goods more and less
exposed to tariffs, and leave out effects through the exchange rate and the Fed's monetary-policy response.

22
00:03:30,767 --> 00:03:38,842
Within those limits, the tariffs look more like a step up in prices
than lasting inflation, though a step that firms are taking slowly.

23
00:03:39,867 --> 00:03:46,892
Next time someone says another country pays a tariff, ask
one question: did that country's sellers cut their prices?

24
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:54,987
And when a tariff changes, don't judge it by next week's receipts;
the goods made at home take the better part of a year to catch up.
